            <ArticleTitle>Restored Physiological Vasomotion Imitating Intrascaffold Restenosis 3 Years After Bioresorbable Scaffold Implantation.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1574.e9-1574.e10</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0828-282X(16)00214-2</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.cjca.2016.02.069</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Bioresorbable vascular scaffolds (BVSs) have recently been introduced into clinical practice; they have many potential advantages, 1 of which includes restoring physiological vasomotion. It has been shown that the degree of vasomotion is correlated with bioresorption. We present a case of intrascaffold spasm masquerading as restenosis 3 years after implantation, despite the ongoing process of bioresorption: intra-BVS vasospasm should therefore be considered and confirmed by intracoronary nitrate administration.</AbstractText>
